{"step_index":140,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-04T17:55:16Z","content":"Priyanshu,\n\nIt can work for **any** Gmail ID, but there is a strict catch you need to know.\n\nWhen I turn the automation bot on for the first time, it will give you a login link. Whichever Gmail ID you log into at that exact moment is where the videos will upload and where the analytics will be pulled from.\n\n**The Catch:**\nBecause your Google Cloud project is brand new and unverified, your credentials are in \"Testing\" mode. Google will automatically block the bot if you try to log in with a different Gmail ID, *unless* you whitelist it first.\n\n**What you must do:**\nIf the YouTube channel you want to automate is on a *different* Gmail ID than the one you used to log into Google Cloud, you must do this:\n1. Go back to the **OAuth consent screen** on the left menu in Google Cloud.\n2. Scroll down to the **Test users** section.\n3. Click **+ Add Users** and type in the exact Gmail ID of your YouTube channel.\n\nIf you are using the exact same Gmail ID for both Google Cloud and your YouTube channel, you don't have to do anything. Download the JSON file and give it to me.","thinking":"**Clarifying the Query**\n\nI'm currently dissecting the user's question, focusing on the nuance between a specific Gmail ID and any Gmail ID for YouTube Data API v3 usage. The user's Google Cloud Console setup is the backdrop.\n\n\n**Generating Credentials**\n\nI'm now zeroing in on the OAuth credential generation, specifically for a desktop application. I'm focusing on the implications of OAuth 2.0. The ability to log in with any Gmail ID at runtime, provided permissions are granted, opens some key opportunities for the user, and may be a limitation. Now I'm considering the edge cases in testing vs. production OAuth consent screens.\n\n\n**Addressing the Caveat**\n\nI've hit a critical point: while technically accepting any Gmail ID is feasible, the \"Testing\" status of the credentials introduces a significant hurdle. My current response emphasizes the necessity of explicitly adding the desired Gmail ID to the \"Test users\" list within the OAuth consent screen. It's a key detail to ensure functionality, and I'm framing it as a direct and essential step for the user.\n\n\n"}
